{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fbd5YsfSngJt7J9tP4Q2bE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — struct_self_consist_ie\n2\t\n3\t> Updated 2026-05-14 post-Dream analysis. Dream dream_20260514T093336 revealed structural issues (3/6 all tiers). Major revision: add strong baselines, reframe contribution, expand evaluation.\n4\t\n5\t## Target Venue\n6\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR May 2026)\n7\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n8\t- Deadline: ARR May cycle, 2026-05-25\n9\t\n10\t## Core Claims (2 条)\n11\t\n12\t### Claim 1 (Positive — NER)\n13\tFor entity-level information extraction, set-aware self-consistency (soft Jaccard over structured outputs) provides a significantly stronger confidence signal than both surface agreement (Fleiss' Kappa) AND cheaper alternatives (sequence log-probability, exact-match voting), enabling effective test-time scaling through consistency-based sample selection.\n14\t\n15\t**Key**: Claim 1 must beat log-probability to justify 16x inference cost. If SJ only beats FK but not log-prob, the claim weakens to \"SJ is a better consistency metric than FK\" (modest contribution). If SJ beats log-prob, the claim becomes \"structural consistency captures information beyond raw model confidence\" (strong contribution).\n16\t\n17\t**MVP evidence**: NER rho_sj=0.388 (SJ>FK +12pp, p=2e-20). Baseline comparisons (log-prob, exact-match, entity voting) pending — these are the make-or-break experiments.\n18\t\n19\t### Claim 2 (Diagnostic — task-complexity boundary)\n20\tThe effectiveness of consistency-based confidence is governed by task structural complexity: compound error propagation in relational tasks fundamentally degrades the consistency-accuracy correlation on the full instance set, but the signal remains valid within the model's capability range. This boundary applies across all confidence signals tested (SJ, FK, log-prob, exact-match).\n21\t\n22\t**MVP evidence**: RE full rho_sj=0.234, RE cond rho_sj=0.372 (SJ>FK +11.8pp). Component decomposition shows all RE components equally weak (head_sj rho=0.210). T=0.7 naturally separates knowledge-gap vs uncertainty errors.\n23\t\n24\t## Story Spine\n25\t\n26\t**Problem**: Test-time scaling (TTS) through sampling + selection requires a reliable confidence signal. Self-consistency has shown promise in reasoning tasks (majority voting), but structured prediction (IE) poses unique challenges: outputs have internal structure (entities, relations, triplets), and it is unclear which confidence signals — model confidence (log-prob), surface agreement (exact-match/FK), or structural similarity (SJ) — best correlate with output quality.\n27\t\n28\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Self-consistency in LLM reasoning uses exact-match voting (Wang et al. 2023). Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al. NAACL 2024) extends this to non-votable outputs via LLM-as-judge. For structured prediction, no systematic comparison exists across confidence signal families (model-internal vs surface vs structural) for TTS selection. Semantic uncertainty methods (Kuhn et al. ICML 2023) cluster outputs by equivalence but don't leverage structural similarity.\n29\t\n30\t**Our framing**: We systematically compare four families of confidence signals for IE test-time scaling: (1) model confidence (sequence log-probability), (2) surface agreement (Fleiss' Kappa, exact-match voting rate), (3) set-aware structural similarity (soft Jaccard), and (4) entity-level majority voting. We discover that set-aware consistency outperforms all alternatives for entity-level tasks, but ALL signals degrade for relational tasks due to compound error propagation. This establishes both the best-practice confidence signal for NER TTS and a principled task-complexity boundary for consistency-based TTS in structured prediction.\n31\t\n32\t**Headline result template**: \"On three NER datasets, set-aware self-consistency (soft Jaccard) achieves rho=0.39-0.45 correlation with accuracy, outperforming sequence log-probability (rho=X), exact-match voting (rho=Y), and Fleiss' Kappa (rho=0.26-0.32), yielding +Zpp F1 via best-of-N selection. However, on relation extraction, all confidence signals degrade (full-set rho<0.28), with conditional analysis revealing a task-complexity boundary governed by compound error propagation.\"\n33\t\n34\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n35\t\n36\t### R1: \"Only one NER dataset — generalization unclear\"\n37\t- **Mitigation**: exp-002 (CoNLL-2003) + exp-003 (WNUT-17) validate cross-dataset across 3 domains (science/news/social media)\n38\t- **Priority**: P0\n39\t\n40\t### R2: \"Only one model family (Qwen3-8B)\"\n41\t- **Mitigation**: exp-007 (LLaMA-3.1-8B) shows model-agnostic effect\n42\t- **Priority**: P0\n43\t\n44\t### R3: \"FK is a strawman baseline — what about log-prob, USC, exact-match?\"\n45\t- **Mitigation**: exp-012 (log-prob), exp-013 (exact-match voting), exp-014 (entity voting) provide comprehensive baseline comparison. USC discussed in related work (requires LLM-as-judge, orthogonal approach).\n46\t- **Priority**: P0, make-or-break\n47\t\n48\t### R4: \"No actual TTS improvement demonstrated\"\n49\t- **Mitigation**: exp-006 (adaptive compute demo) — SJ vs log-prob vs FK vs random best-of-N selection on all NER datasets\n50\t- **Priority**: P0\n51\t\n52\t### R5: \"Sample size N=8 may be too small for reliable SJ estimation\"\n53\t- **Mitigation**: exp-001 (N=16) with multi-seed robustness\n54\t- **Priority**: P0\n55\t\n56\t### R6: \"Conditional analysis cherry-picks favorable instances\"\n57\t- **Mitigation**: Principled separation (knowledge-gap vs uncertainty). Add threshold sensitivity analysis. Temperature analysis provides independent validation.\n58\t- **Priority**: P0 (add sensitivity analysis)\n59\t\n60\t### R7: \"Only ρ as evaluation metric\"\n61\t- **Mitigation**: Add AUROC, calibration plots, Kendall's τ for all experiments\n62\t- **Priority**: P0\n63\t\n64\t### R8: \"Formula-implementation discrepancy\"\n65\t- **Mitigation**: Paper must describe actual algorithm (type-grouped Hungarian matching with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) denominator)\n66\t- **Priority**: P0, paper writing fix\n67\t\n68\t### R9: \"Evaluation circularity (T>0 consistency vs T=0 F1)\"\n69\t- **Mitigation**: Add leave-one-out SJ evaluation as robustness check. Justify cross-mode design (greedy = deterministic reference, standard in TTS literature).\n70\t- **Priority**: P0\n71\t\n72\t### R10: \"Insufficient related work\"\n73\t- **Mitigation**: Expand to 30+ references. Must cite: USC (Chen 2024), semantic uncertainty (Kuhn 2023), calibration (Kadavath 2022, Desai & Durrett 2020), SelfCheckGPT (Manakul 2023), selective prediction (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017).\n74\t- **Priority**: P0, Paper Agent handles\n75\t\n76\t## Venue Fit\n77\t\n78\tEMNLP is well-suited for:\n79\t- Systematic empirical comparison of confidence signals for structured prediction\n80\t- Diagnostic findings with principled explanations\n81\t- The \"comparison + boundary condition\" contribution pattern\n82\t\n83\tRisk: If SJ doesn't beat log-prob, the paper becomes a pure analysis piece. Mitigation: diagnostic framework (Claim 2) remains valid regardless; reframe as \"systematic study of confidence signals for IE TTS.\"\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02166318-33d0-4828-8556-e2b8dcad5370","uuid":"b5845aca-3942-4c13-9184-11ccc8098635","timestamp":"2026-05-14T11:42:24.897Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T105303/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — struct_self_consist_ie\n\n> Updated 2026-05-14 post-Dream analysis. Dream dream_20260514T093336 revealed structural issues (3/6 all tiers). Major revision: add strong baselines, reframe contribution, expand evaluation.\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR May 2026)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR May cycle, 2026-05-25\n\n## Core Claims (2 条)\n\n### Claim 1 (Positive — NER)\nFor entity-level information extraction, set-aware self-consistency (soft Jaccard over structured outputs) provides a significantly stronger confidence signal than both surface agreement (Fleiss' Kappa) AND cheaper alternatives (sequence log-probability, exact-match voting), enabling effective test-time scaling through consistency-based sample selection.\n\n**Key**: Claim 1 must beat log-probability to justify 16x inference cost. If SJ only beats FK but not log-prob, the claim weakens to \"SJ is a better consistency metric than FK\" (modest contribution). If SJ beats log-prob, the claim becomes \"structural consistency captures information beyond raw model confidence\" (strong contribution).\n\n**MVP evidence**: NER rho_sj=0.388 (SJ>FK +12pp, p=2e-20). Baseline comparisons (log-prob, exact-match, entity voting) pending — these are the make-or-break experiments.\n\n### Claim 2 (Diagnostic — task-complexity boundary)\nThe effectiveness of consistency-based confidence is governed by task structural complexity: compound error propagation in relational tasks fundamentally degrades the consistency-accuracy correlation on the full instance set, but the signal remains valid within the model's capability range. This boundary applies across all confidence signals tested (SJ, FK, log-prob, exact-match).\n\n**MVP evidence**: RE full rho_sj=0.234, RE cond rho_sj=0.372 (SJ>FK +11.8pp). Component decomposition shows all RE components equally weak (head_sj rho=0.210). T=0.7 naturally separates knowledge-gap vs uncertainty errors.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Test-time scaling (TTS) through sampling + selection requires a reliable confidence signal. Self-consistency has shown promise in reasoning tasks (majority voting), but structured prediction (IE) poses unique challenges: outputs have internal structure (entities, relations, triplets), and it is unclear which confidence signals — model confidence (log-prob), surface agreement (exact-match/FK), or structural similarity (SJ) — best correlate with output quality.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Self-consistency in LLM reasoning uses exact-match voting (Wang et al. 2023). Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al. NAACL 2024) extends this to non-votable outputs via LLM-as-judge. For structured prediction, no systematic comparison exists across confidence signal families (model-internal vs surface vs structural) for TTS selection. Semantic uncertainty methods (Kuhn et al. ICML 2023) cluster outputs by equivalence but don't leverage structural similarity.\n\n**Our framing**: We systematically compare four families of confidence signals for IE test-time scaling: (1) model confidence (sequence log-probability), (2) surface agreement (Fleiss' Kappa, exact-match voting rate), (3) set-aware structural similarity (soft Jaccard), and (4) entity-level majority voting. We discover that set-aware consistency outperforms all alternatives for entity-level tasks, but ALL signals degrade for relational tasks due to compound error propagation. This establishes both the best-practice confidence signal for NER TTS and a principled task-complexity boundary for consistency-based TTS in structured prediction.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"On three NER datasets, set-aware self-consistency (soft Jaccard) achieves rho=0.39-0.45 correlation with accuracy, outperforming sequence log-probability (rho=X), exact-match voting (rho=Y), and Fleiss' Kappa (rho=0.26-0.32), yielding +Zpp F1 via best-of-N selection. However, on relation extraction, all confidence signals degrade (full-set rho<0.28), with conditional analysis revealing a task-complexity boundary governed by compound error propagation.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### R1: \"Only one NER dataset — generalization unclear\"\n- **Mitigation**: exp-002 (CoNLL-2003) + exp-003 (WNUT-17) validate cross-dataset across 3 domains (science/news/social media)\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R2: \"Only one model family (Qwen3-8B)\"\n- **Mitigation**: exp-007 (LLaMA-3.1-8B) shows model-agnostic effect\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R3: \"FK is a strawman baseline — what about log-prob, USC, exact-match?\"\n- **Mitigation**: exp-012 (log-prob), exp-013 (exact-match voting), exp-014 (entity voting) provide comprehensive baseline comparison. USC discussed in related work (requires LLM-as-judge, orthogonal approach).\n- **Priority**: P0, make-or-break\n\n### R4: \"No actual TTS improvement demonstrated\"\n- **Mitigation**: exp-006 (adaptive compute demo) — SJ vs log-prob vs FK vs random best-of-N selection on all NER datasets\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R5: \"Sample size N=8 may be too small for reliable SJ estimation\"\n- **Mitigation**: exp-001 (N=16) with multi-seed robustness\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R6: \"Conditional analysis cherry-picks favorable instances\"\n- **Mitigation**: Principled separation (knowledge-gap vs uncertainty). Add threshold sensitivity analysis. Temperature analysis provides independent validation.\n- **Priority**: P0 (add sensitivity analysis)\n\n### R7: \"Only ρ as evaluation metric\"\n- **Mitigation**: Add AUROC, calibration plots, Kendall's τ for all experiments\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R8: \"Formula-implementation discrepancy\"\n- **Mitigation**: Paper must describe actual algorithm (type-grouped Hungarian matching with max(|g_a|,|g_b|) denominator)\n- **Priority**: P0, paper writing fix\n\n### R9: \"Evaluation circularity (T>0 consistency vs T=0 F1)\"\n- **Mitigation**: Add leave-one-out SJ evaluation as robustness check. Justify cross-mode design (greedy = deterministic reference, standard in TTS literature).\n- **Priority**: P0\n\n### R10: \"Insufficient related work\"\n- **Mitigation**: Expand to 30+ references. Must cite: USC (Chen 2024), semantic uncertainty (Kuhn 2023), calibration (Kadavath 2022, Desai & Durrett 2020), SelfCheckGPT (Manakul 2023), selective prediction (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017).\n- **Priority**: P0, Paper Agent handles\n\n## Venue Fit\n\nEMNLP is well-suited for:\n- Systematic empirical comparison of confidence signals for structured prediction\n- Diagnostic findings with principled explanations\n- The \"comparison + boundary condition\" contribution pattern\n\nRisk: If SJ doesn't beat log-prob, the paper becomes a pure analysis piece. Mitigation: diagnostic framework (Claim 2) remains valid regardless; reframe as \"systematic study of confidence signals for IE TTS.\"\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}}}
